                  <msItem>
                     <title>J. Glaston. chronica</title>
                     <note type="MLGB3_generalNotes">MLGB's date of s. xv in is superseded by the
                        researches of J. P. Carley, who re-edited the chronicle in 1978 (with
                        English translation, 1985). The chronicle was first edited, using this
                        manuscript, by Thomas Hearne (1726).</note>
                     <note type="MLGB3_generalNotes">The manuscript is described by D. Skemer,
                        Medieval and Renaissance Manuscripts in the Princeton University Library, 2
                        vols (Princeton NJ, 2013), vol. 1, pp. 356-58.</note>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="1497" when="1497" calendar="#gregorian">1497</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_224">Glastonbury, Somerset.
                        Benedictine Abbey of Saint Mary the Virgin</orgName><p>'Liber monasterii
                        beate marie glastonie quem willelmus wych monacus scribi fecit de voluntate
                        Ricardi Bere abbatis anno iiijto' (fol. 141v; cf. 151).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Richard Bere was abbot of Glastonbury,
                     1493-1524.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Lord Charles Bruce, 3rd earl of Aylesbury,
                     1726.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Marquis of Aylesbury in 1858 (see Memorials
                     of Henry V, RS, p. lix).</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Obtained by Robert Garrett in 1928 from
                     Quaritch. Given by him to Princeton in 1942.</provenance>
               </history>
